{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824327068,"sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d7dc560016iscPsWLy19j6K","sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","messageID":"msg_0b9d7d121001ttTAj5NPsvZ0EG","type":"tool","id":"call_00_SWaewGuaYcHj7xfQAIMf2954","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":120,"offset":1340,"path":"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 1340-1459\n1340:  •\n1341:  the review by the Board with the Company’s management and advisors of the structure of the merger and the financial and other terms of the merger agreement, including the merger consideration in relation to the\n1342: current market price of the Company’s common stock and the historical, present and anticipated future operating results and financial position of the Company;\n1343:  •\n1344:  based on the Board’s review of the process conducted by the Transaction Committee, the Board’s belief that $25.00 per share in cash and the terms of the merger agreement offer the best value reasonably\n1345: attainable for the Company’s shareholders; \n1346:  •\n1347:  the fact that the value of the merger consideration of $25.00 per share in cash represents a 53% premium over the per share closing price of the Company’s common stock of $16.30 on August 12, 2016, the last trading\n1348: day concluded prior to the Company and G&W entering into the merger agreement; \n1349:  •\n1350:  the fact that the merger consideration would be paid solely in cash, which, compared to non-cash consideration, provides certainty and immediate liquidity and value to our shareholders, enabling our shareholders to\n1351: realize value that has been created at the Company while eliminating long-term business and execution risk; \n1352:  •\n1353:  the fact that the merger agreement was the product of arm’s—length negotiations and contained terms and conditions that were, in the Board’s view, advisable and favorable to the Company and its\n1354: shareholders; \n1355:  •\n1356:  the timing of the merger and the risk that if the Company did not accept G&W’s offer at the time that it did, the Board might not have had another opportunity to do so; \n1357:  •\n1358:  the fact that the merger is subject to the approval of the merger agreement by the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of the Company’s common stock and preferred stock, each voting separately\n1359: as a class, entitled to vote thereon; \n1360:  •\n1361:  the terms of the merger agreement and the related agreements, including: \n1362:  •\n1363:  the limited number and nature of the conditions to G&W’s obligation to consummate the merger; \n1364:  33 \n1365:  Table of Contents \n1366:  •\n1367:  the Company’s ability, prior to the time that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and plan of merger, to discuss and negotiate, under specified circumstances, an unsolicited bona fide\n1368: written takeover proposal should one be made and, if the Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its legal and financial advisors, that the unsolicited bona fide written takeover proposal constitutes, or is reasonably likely to lead\n1369: to, a superior proposal and that the failure to pursue such superior proposal would be inconsistent with the Board’s fiduciary duties under applicable law, the Board is permitted to terminate the merger agreement in order to enter into a\n1370: definitive agreement for that superior proposal, subject to certain notice requirements, “matching rights” and payment of a termination fee of $3.785 million to G&W; \n1371:  •\n1372:  the belief of the Board that, although the termination fee might have the effect of discouraging competing third-party proposals, such provisions are customary for transactions of this type, and the Board determined\n1373: that the $3.785 million termination fee was reasonable in light of, among other things, the benefits of the merger to the Company’s shareholders, the typical size of such fees in similar transactions and the likelihood that a fee of such size\n1374: would not preclude or unreasonably restrict the emergence of alternative takeover proposals; \n1375:  •\n1376:  the Board’s ability, prior to the time that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and plan of merger, to change or withdraw its recommendation of the merger agreement in response to a\n1377: superior proposal or any positive change, event, or occurrence with respect to the Company’s business that becomes known to the Board and was not known to the Board when the merger agreement was entered into, in each case, if the Board\n1378: determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to change or withdraw its recommendation would be inconsistent with the Board’s fiduciary duties under applicable law, subject to certain notice\n1379: requirements and “matching rights”; \n1380:  •\n1381:  the likelihood that the merger will be completed, based on, among other things: \n1382:  •\n1383:  the absence of a financing condition in the merger agreement and the representation of G&W in the merger agreement as to its financial wherewithal; \n1384:  •\n1385:  the conditions to closing contained in the merger agreement, which are customary in number and scope, and which, in the case of the condition related to the accuracy of the Company’s representations and warranties,\n1386: are generally subject to a “Company Material Adverse Effect” qualification; \n1387:  •\n1388:  the likelihood that the regulatory and shareholder approvals needed to complete the merger will be obtained in a timely fashion and the agreement of G&W in the merger agreement to seek STB approval to close into a\n1389: voting trust, if necessary, to ensure that the merger can be completed as promptly as possible and ensure that the risk of regulatory delays is not borne by the Company or its shareholders; \n1390:  •\n1391:  the financial analysis reviewed by representatives of BMO with the Board as well as the oral opinion of BMO rendered to the Board, which was subsequently confirmed by delivery of a written opinion, dated August 12,\n1392: 2016, to the effect that as of that date, and based upon and subject to the various assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of review undertaken by BMO as set forth in its written\n1393: opinion, the merger consideration to be received by the holders of our common stock in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement was fair, from a financial point of view, to those holders (other than the Eder Trusts), as more fully described below\n1394: under “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor” beginning on page 36; and \n1395:  •\n1396:  the experience and reputation of G&W in completing transactions of this type. \n1397:  34 \n1398:  Table of Contents \n1399:  The Board also considered a variety of potentially negative factors in its deliberations\n1400: concerning the merger agreement and the merger, including, among others, the following: \n1401:  •\n1402:  the challenges associated with seeking the regulatory approvals required to complete the merger in a timely manner; \n1403:  •\n1404:  the significant costs associated with entering into the merger agreement and completing the merger, the substantial time and effort of management required to complete the merger, the potential for diversion of\n1405: management and employee attention, and for employee attrition, during the period prior to the completion of the merger and the potential effect on the Company’s business and relations with customers, service providers and other stakeholders,\n1406: whether or not the merger is completed; \n1407:  •\n1408:  the requirement that the Company conduct its business in the ordinary course and the other restrictions on the conduct of the Company’s business prior to completion of the merger, which may delay or prevent the\n1409: Company from undertaking business opportunities that may arise pending completion of the merger; \n1410:  •\n1411:  the possibility, though unlikely from the Company’s perspective, that all conditions to the parties’ obligations to complete the merger will not be satisfied, and as a result, it is possible that the merger\n1412: does not close (including as a result of events outside of either party’s control), even if the merger agreement is approved by the Company’s shareholders; \n1413:  •\n1414:  the fact that the Company will lose the autonomy associated with being an independent railroad; \n1415:  •\n1416:  the fact that the shareholders will have no ongoing equity participation in the Company following the merger, and that such shareholders will cease to participate in the Company’s future earnings or growth, if any,\n1417: and will not participate in any potential future sale of the Company to a third party; \n1418:  •\n1419:  the fact that certain provisions of the merger agreement prohibit the Company from soliciting, and limit its ability to respond to, proposals for alternative transactions; \n1420:  •\n1421:  the fact that the merger agreement entitles G&W to terminate the merger agreement if, among other things, the Company commences negotiations regarding an alternative acquisition proposal in material breach of the\n1422: non-solicitation prohibitions under the merger agreement, and obligates the Company to pay to G&W a termination fee of $3.785 million (approximately 3% of the transaction value) if the Company recommends or accepts an alternative acquisition\n1423: proposal, which may deter others from proposing an alternative transaction that may be more advantageous to the Company’s shareholders; and \n1424:  •\n1425:  the receipt of cash by shareholders in exchange for their common stock and preferred stock pursuant to the merger will be a taxable transaction to our shareholders for U.S. federal income tax purposes.\n1426:  The discussion of the information and factors considered by the Board is not exhaustive, but includes all material\n1427: factors considered by the Board. In view of the wide variety of factors considered by the Board in connection with its evaluation of the merger and the complexity of these matters, the Board did not consider it practical to, nor did it attempt to,\n1428: quantify, rank or otherwise assign relative weights to the specific factors that it considered in reaching its decision. The Board evaluated the factors described above with the Company’s management and legal and financial advisors. In\n1429: considering the factors described above, individual members of the Board may have given different weights to different factors. The Board realized there can be no assurance about future results, including results expected or considered in the\n1430: factors listed above. However, the Board concluded the potential positive factors outweighed the potential negative factors of completing the merger. It should be noted that this explanation of the Board’s reasoning and all other information\n1431: presented in this section is forward-looking in nature and, therefore, should be read in light of the factors discussed in the section of this proxy statement titled “Cautionary Statement Regarding Forward-Looking Statements” beginning on\n1432: page 20. \n1433:  During its consideration of the merger described above, the Board was also aware that some of its directors and executive\n1434: officers may have interests in the merger that are different from or in addition to those of its \n1435:  35 \n1436:  Table of Contents \n1437: shareholders generally, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger”\n1438: beginning on page 44. \n1439:  The Board (i) determined that the merger agreement, the plan of merger and the merger and other transactions\n1440: contemplated by the merger agreement are fair to, advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its shareholders, (ii) approved and declared advisable the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby, including the merger and\n1441: the plan of merger, (iii) directed that the merger agreement be submitted to the Company’s shareholders for approval and (iv) resolved to recommend that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and the transactions\n1442: contemplated thereby, including the merger and the plan of merger. Accordingly, the Board (other than Director Smith, who recused himself from the August 12, 2016 meeting) approved and declared advisable the merger agreement and the transactions\n1443: contemplated thereby (including the merger and the plan of merger). \n1444:  THE BOARD OF DIRECTORS RECOMMENDS THAT THE SHAREHOLDERS VOTE\n1445: “FOR” APPROVAL OF THE MERGER AGREEMENT AND THE TRANSACTIONS CONTEMPLATED THEREBY, INCLUDING THE MERGER AND THE PLAN OF MERGER ATTACHED AS EXHIBIT A TO THE MERGER AGREEMENT. \n1446:  Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor \n1447:  We retained GHF, the business of which was subsequently acquired by BMO, an affiliate of Bank of Montreal, to act as our financial advisor in\n1448: connection with the merger. In selecting GHF, we considered, among other things, the fact that GHF was a reputable investment banking firm with substantial experience advising companies in the rail sector and in providing strategic advisory services\n1449: in general, GHF had recent experience providing strategic advisory services in connection with the sale of a short-line railroad and, in the Board’s opinion, GHF would commit appropriate attention, resources and personnel to the process. \n1450:  On August 12, 2016, BMO rendered an oral opinion to the Board, which was subsequently confirmed in a written opinion as of the same date, as\n1451: to the fairness, from a financial point of view, to the holders of our common stock (other than the Eder Trusts) as of that date, and based upon and subject to the assumptions made, matters considered and limitations and qualifications upon the\n1452: review undertaken by BMO, of the merger consideration per share of our common stock to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n1453:  The full text of BMO’s written opinion, dated August 12, 2016, is attached to this proxy statement as\n1454:  Annex C and is incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. You should read BMO’s opinion carefully and in its entirety for a discussion of, among other things, the\n1455: scope of the review undertaken and the assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations upon the review undertaken by BMO in connection with its opinion. This summary is qualified in its entirety by\n1456: reference to the full text of the opinion. BMO’s opinion was directed to the Board, in its capacity as the Board, and addressed only the fairness from a financial point of view, as of the date of the opinion, to the holders of our common stock\n1457: (other than the Eder Trusts) of the consideration to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n1458:  In connection with rendering its opinion, BMO, among other things: \n1459:  •\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1460]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 1340-1459\n1340:  •\n1341:  the review by the Board with the Company’s management and advisors of the structure of the merger and the financial and other terms of the merger agreement, including the merger consideration in relation to the\n1342: current market price of the Company’s common stock and the historical, present and anticipated future operating results and financial position of the Company;\n1343:  •\n1344:  based on the Board’s review of the process conducted by the Transaction Committee, the Board’s belief that $25.00 per share in cash and the terms of the merger agreement offer the best value reasonably\n1345: attainable for the Company’s shareholders; \n1346:  •\n1347:  the fact that the value of the merger consideration of $25.00 per share in cash represents a 53% premium over the per share closing price of the Company’s common stock of $16.30 on August 12, 2016, the last trading\n1348: day concluded prior to the Company and G&W entering into the merger agreement; \n1349:  •\n1350:  the fact that the merger consideration would be paid solely in cash, which, compared to non-cash consideration, provides certainty and immediate liquidity and value to our shareholders, enabling our shareholders to\n1351: realize value that has been created at the Company while eliminating long-term business and execution risk; \n1352:  •\n1353:  the fact that the merger agreement was the product of arm’s—length negotiations and contained terms and conditions that were, in the Board’s view, advisable and favorable to the Company and its\n1354: shareholders; \n1355:  •\n1356:  the timing of the merger and the risk that if the Company did not accept G&W’s offer at the time that it did, the Board might not have had another opportunity to do so; \n1357:  •\n1358:  the fact that the merger is subject to the approval of the merger agreement by the affirmative vote of a majority of the outstanding shares of the Company’s common stock and preferred stock, each voting separately\n1359: as a class, entitled to vote thereon; \n1360:  •\n1361:  the terms of the merger agreement and the related agreements, including: \n1362:  •\n1363:  the limited number and nature of the conditions to G&W’s obligation to consummate the merger; \n1364:  33 \n1365:  Table of Contents \n1366:  •\n1367:  the Company’s ability, prior to the time that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and plan of merger, to discuss and negotiate, under specified circumstances, an unsolicited bona fide\n1368: written takeover proposal should one be made and, if the Board determines in good faith, after consultation with its legal and financial advisors, that the unsolicited bona fide written takeover proposal constitutes, or is reasonably likely to lead\n1369: to, a superior proposal and that the failure to pursue such superior proposal would be inconsistent with the Board’s fiduciary duties under applicable law, the Board is permitted to terminate the merger agreement in order to enter into a\n1370: definitive agreement for that superior proposal, subject to certain notice requirements, “matching rights” and payment of a termination fee of $3.785 million to G&W; \n1371:  •\n1372:  the belief of the Board that, although the termination fee might have the effect of discouraging competing third-party proposals, such provisions are customary for transactions of this type, and the Board determined\n1373: that the $3.785 million termination fee was reasonable in light of, among other things, the benefits of the merger to the Company’s shareholders, the typical size of such fees in similar transactions and the likelihood that a fee of such size\n1374: would not preclude or unreasonably restrict the emergence of alternative takeover proposals; \n1375:  •\n1376:  the Board’s ability, prior to the time that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and plan of merger, to change or withdraw its recommendation of the merger agreement in response to a\n1377: superior proposal or any positive change, event, or occurrence with respect to the Company’s business that becomes known to the Board and was not known to the Board when the merger agreement was entered into, in each case, if the Board\n1378: determines in good faith, after consultation with its outside legal counsel, that the failure to change or withdraw its recommendation would be inconsistent with the Board’s fiduciary duties under applicable law, subject to certain notice\n1379: requirements and “matching rights”; \n1380:  •\n1381:  the likelihood that the merger will be completed, based on, among other things: \n1382:  •\n1383:  the absence of a financing condition in the merger agreement and the representation of G&W in the merger agreement as to its financial wherewithal; \n1384:  •\n1385:  the conditions to closing contained in the merger agreement, which are customary in number and scope, and which, in the case of the condition related to the accuracy of the Company’s representations and warranties,\n1386: are generally subject to a “Company Material Adverse Effect” qualification; \n1387:  •\n1388:  the likelihood that the regulatory and shareholder approvals needed to complete the merger will be obtained in a timely fashion and the agreement of G&W in the merger agreement to seek STB approval to close into a\n1389: voting trust, if necessary, to ensure that the merger can be completed as promptly as possible and ensure that the risk of regulatory delays is not borne by the Company or its shareholders; \n1390:  •\n1391:  the financial analysis reviewed by representatives of BMO with the Board as well as the oral opinion of BMO rendered to the Board, which was subsequently confirmed by delivery of a written opinion, dated August 12,\n1392: 2016, to the effect that as of that date, and based upon and subject to the various assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations on the scope of review undertaken by BMO as set forth in its written\n1393: opinion, the merger consideration to be received by the holders of our common stock in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement was fair, from a financial point of view, to those holders (other than the Eder Trusts), as more fully described below\n1394: under “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor” beginning on page 36; and \n1395:  •\n1396:  the experience and reputation of G&W in completing transactions of this type. \n1397:  34 \n1398:  Table of Contents \n1399:  The Board also considered a variety of potentially negative factors in its deliberations\n1400: concerning the merger agreement and the merger, including, among others, the following: \n1401:  •\n1402:  the challenges associated with seeking the regulatory approvals required to complete the merger in a timely manner; \n1403:  •\n1404:  the significant costs associated with entering into the merger agreement and completing the merger, the substantial time and effort of management required to complete the merger, the potential for diversion of\n1405: management and employee attention, and for employee attrition, during the period prior to the completion of the merger and the potential effect on the Company’s business and relations with customers, service providers and other stakeholders,\n1406: whether or not the merger is completed; \n1407:  •\n1408:  the requirement that the Company conduct its business in the ordinary course and the other restrictions on the conduct of the Company’s business prior to completion of the merger, which may delay or prevent the\n1409: Company from undertaking business opportunities that may arise pending completion of the merger; \n1410:  •\n1411:  the possibility, though unlikely from the Company’s perspective, that all conditions to the parties’ obligations to complete the merger will not be satisfied, and as a result, it is possible that the merger\n1412: does not close (including as a result of events outside of either party’s control), even if the merger agreement is approved by the Company’s shareholders; \n1413:  •\n1414:  the fact that the Company will lose the autonomy associated with being an independent railroad; \n1415:  •\n1416:  the fact that the shareholders will have no ongoing equity participation in the Company following the merger, and that such shareholders will cease to participate in the Company’s future earnings or growth, if any,\n1417: and will not participate in any potential future sale of the Company to a third party; \n1418:  •\n1419:  the fact that certain provisions of the merger agreement prohibit the Company from soliciting, and limit its ability to respond to, proposals for alternative transactions; \n1420:  •\n1421:  the fact that the merger agreement entitles G&W to terminate the merger agreement if, among other things, the Company commences negotiations regarding an alternative acquisition proposal in material breach of the\n1422: non-solicitation prohibitions under the merger agreement, and obligates the Company to pay to G&W a termination fee of $3.785 million (approximately 3% of the transaction value) if the Company recommends or accepts an alternative acquisition\n1423: proposal, which may deter others from proposing an alternative transaction that may be more advantageous to the Company’s shareholders; and \n1424:  •\n1425:  the receipt of cash by shareholders in exchange for their common stock and preferred stock pursuant to the merger will be a taxable transaction to our shareholders for U.S. federal income tax purposes.\n1426:  The discussion of the information and factors considered by the Board is not exhaustive, but includes all material\n1427: factors considered by the Board. In view of the wide variety of factors considered by the Board in connection with its evaluation of the merger and the complexity of these matters, the Board did not consider it practical to, nor did it attempt to,\n1428: quantify, rank or otherwise assign relative weights to the specific factors that it considered in reaching its decision. The Board evaluated the factors described above with the Company’s management and legal and financial advisors. In\n1429: considering the factors described above, individual members of the Board may have given different weights to different factors. The Board realized there can be no assurance about future results, including results expected or considered in the\n1430: factors listed above. However, the Board concluded the potential positive factors outweighed the potential negative factors of completing the merger. It should be noted that this explanation of the Board’s reasoning and all other information\n1431: presented in this section is forward-looking in nature and, therefore, should be read in light of the factors discussed in the section of this proxy statement titled “Cautionary Statement Regarding Forward-Looking Statements” beginning on\n1432: page 20. \n1433:  During its consideration of the merger described above, the Board was also aware that some of its directors and executive\n1434: officers may have interests in the merger that are different from or in addition to those of its \n1435:  35 \n1436:  Table of Contents \n1437: shareholders generally, as described in the section of this proxy statement titled “Proposal No. 1—The Merger—Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger”\n1438: beginning on page 44. \n1439:  The Board (i) determined that the merger agreement, the plan of merger and the merger and other transactions\n1440: contemplated by the merger agreement are fair to, advisable and in the best interests of the Company and its shareholders, (ii) approved and declared advisable the merger agreement and the transactions contemplated thereby, including the merger and\n1441: the plan of merger, (iii) directed that the merger agreement be submitted to the Company’s shareholders for approval and (iv) resolved to recommend that the Company’s shareholders approve the merger agreement and the transactions\n1442: contemplated thereby, including the merger and the plan of merger. Accordingly, the Board (other than Director Smith, who recused himself from the August 12, 2016 meeting) approved and declared advisable the merger agreement and the transactions\n1443: contemplated thereby (including the merger and the plan of merger). \n1444:  THE BOARD OF DIRECTORS RECOMMENDS THAT THE SHAREHOLDERS VOTE\n1445: “FOR” APPROVAL OF THE MERGER AGREEMENT AND THE TRANSACTIONS CONTEMPLATED THEREBY, INCLUDING THE MERGER AND THE PLAN OF MERGER ATTACHED AS EXHIBIT A TO THE MERGER AGREEMENT. \n1446:  Opinion of the Company’s Financial Advisor \n1447:  We retained GHF, the business of which was subsequently acquired by BMO, an affiliate of Bank of Montreal, to act as our financial advisor in\n1448: connection with the merger. In selecting GHF, we considered, among other things, the fact that GHF was a reputable investment banking firm with substantial experience advising companies in the rail sector and in providing strategic advisory services\n1449: in general, GHF had recent experience providing strategic advisory services in connection with the sale of a short-line railroad and, in the Board’s opinion, GHF would commit appropriate attention, resources and personnel to the process. \n1450:  On August 12, 2016, BMO rendered an oral opinion to the Board, which was subsequently confirmed in a written opinion as of the same date, as\n1451: to the fairness, from a financial point of view, to the holders of our common stock (other than the Eder Trusts) as of that date, and based upon and subject to the assumptions made, matters considered and limitations and qualifications upon the\n1452: review undertaken by BMO, of the merger consideration per share of our common stock to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n1453:  The full text of BMO’s written opinion, dated August 12, 2016, is attached to this proxy statement as\n1454:  Annex C and is incorporated into this proxy statement by reference. You should read BMO’s opinion carefully and in its entirety for a discussion of, among other things, the\n1455: scope of the review undertaken and the assumptions made, procedures followed, matters considered and qualifications and limitations upon the review undertaken by BMO in connection with its opinion. This summary is qualified in its entirety by\n1456: reference to the full text of the opinion. BMO’s opinion was directed to the Board, in its capacity as the Board, and addressed only the fairness from a financial point of view, as of the date of the opinion, to the holders of our common stock\n1457: (other than the Eder Trusts) of the consideration to be received by those shareholders in the merger pursuant to the merger agreement. \n1458:  In connection with rendering its opinion, BMO, among other things: \n1459:  •\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1460]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 120, \"offset\": 1340, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824326742,"end":1789824327068}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824327088,"sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d7dcea001IuxybUeAsFhMtv","sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","messageID":"msg_0b9d7d121001ttTAj5NPsvZ0EG","type":"tool","id":"call_01_fFSvf2M4zJ3poz0KOX1J2752","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":40,"offset":4670,"path":"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 4670-4709\n4670:  Table of Contents \n4671:  “ Company Required Vote ” shall mean the affirmative vote of the holders of at\n4672: least a majority of the outstanding shares of Company Common Stock and Company Preferred Stock, each voting separately as a class, in favor of approval of this Agreement and the Plan of Merger. \n4673:  “ Company Shareholders Meeting ” shall mean a meeting of the Company Shareholders to be called to consider the Merger. \n4674:  “ Company Stock Rights ” shall mean any options, warrants, convertible securities, subscriptions, stock appreciation rights,\n4675: phantom stock plans or stock equivalents or other rights, agreements, arrangements or commitments (contingent or otherwise) obligating the Company to issue or sell any shares of capital stock of, or options, warrants, convertible securities,\n4676: subscriptions or other equity interests in, the Company. \n4677:  “ Company Termination Fee ” shall mean $3,785,000. \n4678:  “ Company Transaction Expenses ” shall mean all fees, costs, expenses and disbursements incurred, payable or paid at or prior\n4679: to Closing by or on behalf of any of the Company or its Subsidiaries in connection with the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, including the fees and expenses of any legal counsel, accountants or other professional advisors (including the\n4680: Company Financial Advisor). \n4681:  “ Confidentiality Agreement ” shall mean the Confidentiality Agreement between the Company and\n4682: Parent dated April 3, 2016. \n4683:  “ Contract ” shall mean any loan, guarantee of indebtedness or credit agreement,\n4684: debenture, note, bond, mortgage, indenture, guarantee, license, deed of trust, lease, purchase or sale order or other contract, commitment, agreement, instrument, binding obligation, arrangement, understanding, undertaking, permit, concession or\n4685: franchise, whether written or oral (each, including all amendments, modifications, supplements or restatements thereto). \n4686:  “ Dissenter Shares ” shall mean Preferred Shares issued and outstanding immediately prior to the Effective Time that are held\n4687: by any holder who is entitled to demand and makes a proper demand for payment of the fair value of such Preferred Shares pursuant to, and who complies in all respects with, the provisions of Part 12. \n4688:  “ Effect ” shall mean any occurrence, condition, change, event, effect, development or circumstance. \n4689:  “ Encumbrance ” shall mean any lien, mortgage, pledge, deed of trust, security interest, charge, encumbrance or other adverse\n4690: claim or interest. \n4691:  “ Environmental Laws ” shall mean any Laws or any agreement with any Governmental Entity or other third\n4692: party, relating to the environment, any hazardous or toxic substance, waste or material, or the effect of any of the foregoing on human health and safety. \n4693:  “ Environmental Permits ” shall mean all permits, licenses, franchises, certificates, approvals, registrations and other\n4694: similar authorizations of Governmental Entities relating to or required by Environmental Laws and affecting, or relating to, the business of the Company or any Subsidiary as currently conducted. \n4695:  “ ERISA ” shall mean the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, as amended. \n4696:  “ ESPP ” shall mean the Company’s Employee Stock Purchase Plan originally adopted as of April 1, 1997, as in effect\n4697: on the date hereof. \n4698:  “ Exchange Act ” shall mean the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, and the rules and\n4699: regulations promulgated thereunder. \n4700:  A-48 \n4701:  Table of Contents \n4702:  “ GAAP ” shall mean United States generally accepted accounting principles in\n4703: effect from time to time. \n4704:  “ Governmental Entity ” shall mean any United States federal, state or local or any foreign\n4705: government or any court of competent jurisdiction, administrative or regulatory agency or commission or other governmental authority or agency, domestic or foreign, including any taxing authority and the United States Railroad Retirement Board. \n4706:  “ Hazardous Substance ” shall mean (i) petroleum and petroleum by-products, asbestos or asbestos-containing materials,\n4707: radioactive materials, medical or infectious wastes, or polychlorinated biphenyls, and (ii) any other material, substance or waste that is prohibited, limited or regulated under, or that can give rise to liability under, any Environmental Law.\n4708:  “ HSR Act ” shall mean the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976, as amended, and the rules and regulations\n4709: thereunder. \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 4710]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 4670-4709\n4670:  Table of Contents \n4671:  “ Company Required Vote ” shall mean the affirmative vote of the holders of at\n4672: least a majority of the outstanding shares of Company Common Stock and Company Preferred Stock, each voting separately as a class, in favor of approval of this Agreement and the Plan of Merger. \n4673:  “ Company Shareholders Meeting ” shall mean a meeting of the Company Shareholders to be called to consider the Merger. \n4674:  “ Company Stock Rights ” shall mean any options, warrants, convertible securities, subscriptions, stock appreciation rights,\n4675: phantom stock plans or stock equivalents or other rights, agreements, arrangements or commitments (contingent or otherwise) obligating the Company to issue or sell any shares of capital stock of, or options, warrants, convertible securities,\n4676: subscriptions or other equity interests in, the Company. \n4677:  “ Company Termination Fee ” shall mean $3,785,000. \n4678:  “ Company Transaction Expenses ” shall mean all fees, costs, expenses and disbursements incurred, payable or paid at or prior\n4679: to Closing by or on behalf of any of the Company or its Subsidiaries in connection with the transactions contemplated by this Agreement, including the fees and expenses of any legal counsel, accountants or other professional advisors (including the\n4680: Company Financial Advisor). \n4681:  “ Confidentiality Agreement ” shall mean the Confidentiality Agreement between the Company and\n4682: Parent dated April 3, 2016. \n4683:  “ Contract ” shall mean any loan, guarantee of indebtedness or credit agreement,\n4684: debenture, note, bond, mortgage, indenture, guarantee, license, deed of trust, lease, purchase or sale order or other contract, commitment, agreement, instrument, binding obligation, arrangement, understanding, undertaking, permit, concession or\n4685: franchise, whether written or oral (each, including all amendments, modifications, supplements or restatements thereto). \n4686:  “ Dissenter Shares ” shall mean Preferred Shares issued and outstanding immediately prior to the Effective Time that are held\n4687: by any holder who is entitled to demand and makes a proper demand for payment of the fair value of such Preferred Shares pursuant to, and who complies in all respects with, the provisions of Part 12. \n4688:  “ Effect ” shall mean any occurrence, condition, change, event, effect, development or circumstance. \n4689:  “ Encumbrance ” shall mean any lien, mortgage, pledge, deed of trust, security interest, charge, encumbrance or other adverse\n4690: claim or interest. \n4691:  “ Environmental Laws ” shall mean any Laws or any agreement with any Governmental Entity or other third\n4692: party, relating to the environment, any hazardous or toxic substance, waste or material, or the effect of any of the foregoing on human health and safety. \n4693:  “ Environmental Permits ” shall mean all permits, licenses, franchises, certificates, approvals, registrations and other\n4694: similar authorizations of Governmental Entities relating to or required by Environmental Laws and affecting, or relating to, the business of the Company or any Subsidiary as currently conducted. \n4695:  “ ERISA ” shall mean the Employee Retirement Income Security Act of 1974, as amended. \n4696:  “ ESPP ” shall mean the Company’s Employee Stock Purchase Plan originally adopted as of April 1, 1997, as in effect\n4697: on the date hereof. \n4698:  “ Exchange Act ” shall mean the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, as amended, and the rules and\n4699: regulations promulgated thereunder. \n4700:  A-48 \n4701:  Table of Contents \n4702:  “ GAAP ” shall mean United States generally accepted accounting principles in\n4703: effect from time to time. \n4704:  “ Governmental Entity ” shall mean any United States federal, state or local or any foreign\n4705: government or any court of competent jurisdiction, administrative or regulatory agency or commission or other governmental authority or agency, domestic or foreign, including any taxing authority and the United States Railroad Retirement Board. \n4706:  “ Hazardous Substance ” shall mean (i) petroleum and petroleum by-products, asbestos or asbestos-containing materials,\n4707: radioactive materials, medical or infectious wastes, or polychlorinated biphenyls, and (ii) any other material, substance or waste that is prohibited, limited or regulated under, or that can give rise to liability under, any Environmental Law.\n4708:  “ HSR Act ” shall mean the Hart-Scott-Rodino Antitrust Improvements Act of 1976, as amended, and the rules and regulations\n4709: thereunder. \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 4710]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 40, \"offset\": 4670, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824326890,"end":1789824327088}}}}
