{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1789824413100,"sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","part":{"partID":"prt_0b9d92c0e001W8ZyVSGsyMmBTr","sessionID":"ses_f4628b113ffe9gyXGlLM5DZwK2","messageID":"msg_0b9d90771001b7AvxludU66xMm","type":"tool","id":"call_00_dXYC0c1zQq9UDtplE2xz9289","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"limit":130,"offset":1740,"path":"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt"},"output":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 1740-1869\n1740:  2.1\n1741:  ( 0.5\n1742:  ) \n1743:  0.2\n1744:  1.4\n1745:  2.8\n1746:  Unlevered Free Cash Flow (c) \n1747:  (1.7\n1748:  ) \n1749:  ( 0.5\n1750:  ) \n1751:  ( 0.1\n1752:  ) \n1753:  1.4\n1754:  1.9\n1755:  (a) \n1756:  Fiscal 2016 data represents only third and fourth quarter 2016 financial information. Costs related to the merger were not included in fiscal 2016 third and fourth quarter financial information. \n1757:  (b) \n1758:  Tax-Affected EBIT represents Earnings before interest and income taxes (“EBIT”) less cash taxes calculated by multiplying 35% marginal tax rate by EBIT after adding back items which are not tax deductible such\n1759: as non-deductible amortization. EBIT is a non-GAAP measure consisting of net income plus interest expense and provision for income taxes. \n1760:  (c) \n1761:  Unlevered Free Cash Flow represents Tax-Affected EBIT plus depreciation and amortization and minus capital\n1762: expenditures and any increase in net working capital. Capital expenditures for fiscal years 2017-2020 have been normalized at 83.6% of depreciation based on 2.5% inflation and a 15-year depreciation period. \n1763:  Delisting and Deregistration of Common Stock Following the Merger \n1764:  If the merger is completed, the Company’s common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Exchange Act and we\n1765: will no longer file periodic reports with the SEC on account of the common stock. \n1766:  43 \n1767:  Table of Contents \n1768:  Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger \n1769:  In considering the recommendation of the Board that you vote to approve the merger agreement, you should be aware that directors and executive\n1770: officers of the Company have financial interests in the merger that may be different from, or in addition to, those of the Company’s shareholders generally. The independent members of the Board were aware of and considered these potential\n1771: interests, among other matters. \n1772:  As described in more detail below, these interests include certain payments and benefits that may be\n1773: provided to directors and executive officers of the Company upon completion of the merger or upon termination of their employment under certain circumstances following the merger, including accelerated vesting of stock options and restricted stock\n1774: units, cash severance in the case of a qualifying termination of employment in connection with or following a change in control (which will include the completion of the merger), and continued indemnification and insurance coverage under the merger\n1775: agreement. \n1776:  The dates and share prices used below to quantify these interests have been selected based upon applicable disclosure\n1777: requirements and are for illustrative purposes only. They do not necessarily reflect the dates on which certain events will occur and do not represent a projection about the future value of common stock. \n1778:  Share Ownership of Directors and Executive Officers \n1779:  As of September 16, 2016, the record date for the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders, the directors and executive officers of\n1780: the Company may be deemed to be the beneficial owners of 1,107,002 shares of common stock (excluding common stock issuable upon conversion of the preferred stock), representing 22.7% of the outstanding shares of common stock, and 500 shares of\n1781: preferred stock, representing 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock. \n1782:  Indemnification \n1783:  The merger agreement provides that the surviving corporation will indemnify all past and present officers and directors of the Company and its\n1784: subsidiaries for acts or omissions related to such person’s service as a director or officer of the Company or its subsidiaries occurring at or prior to the effective time of the merger to the fullest extent permitted by the RIBCA, any other\n1785: applicable law, the Company’s articles of incorporation or the Company’s bylaws, subject to any limitation imposed by law. \n1786:  Under the merger agreement, G&W has agreed that all rights to indemnification and all limitations of liability existing in favor of any\n1787: director or officer of the Company or any of its subsidiaries, as provided in the articles of incorporation and bylaws of the Company, similar governing documents of a Company subsidiary or in applicable law as in effect on the date of the merger\n1788: agreement with respect to matters occurring on or prior to the effective time of the merger will survive the merger and be assumed by the surviving corporation. \n1789:  For a period of six years following the effective time of the merger, the articles of incorporation and the bylaws of the surviving\n1790: corporation will contain provisions no less favorable with respect to indemnification, advancement of expenses and exculpation of former or present directors and officers than as set forth in the articles of incorporation and bylaws of the Company\n1791: as of the date of the merger agreement, which provisions will not be amended, repealed or otherwise modified for six years following the effective time of the merger in any manner that would adversely affect the rights thereunder, except as may be\n1792: required by the RIBCA. \n1793:  Directors’ and Officers’ Insurance \n1794:  The merger agreement provides for G&W to cause the Company to maintain in effect its directors’ and officers’ liability insurance\n1795: coverage existing prior to the effective time of the merger on terms with respect to such coverage and amount no less favorable than those of such current insurance coverage for acts or omissions occurring at or prior to the effective time of the\n1796: merger for a period of six years following the effective time of \n1797:  44 \n1798:  Table of Contents \n1799: the merger. Neither G&W nor the surviving corporation will be obligated to expend in any one year an amount in excess of 300% of the annual premium paid by the Company for such insurance on\n1800: the date of the merger agreement. If the annual premiums for such insurance coverage exceed such amount, G&W and the Company will be obligated to obtain a policy with the greatest coverage available for a cost not exceeding such amount. In lieu\n1801: of the foregoing insurance coverage, G&W has the option to purchase, prior to the effective time of the merger, six year “tail” insurance coverage that provides coverage identical in all material respects to the coverage described\n1802: above, provided that the premium is not more than 300% of the annual premium paid by the Company for such insurance on the date of the merger agreement. \n1803:  The indemnification and insurance provisions of the merger agreement described above are intended to benefit, and are enforceable by, the\n1804: indemnified persons and their respective heirs or representatives. \n1805:  Change in Control Benefits Under Current Company Agreements \n1806:  Change in Control Agreements with Company Executives. The Company has entered into change in control severance agreements\n1807: with its executive officers (other than Mr. Eder), including named executive officers P. Scott Conti and Frank K. Rogers. The change in control severance agreements provide that, if the executive’s employment is terminated by the Company\n1808: without “cause” or by the executive for “good reason” (each as defined in the agreements) within two years after a change in control, the executive is entitled to a lump sum cash payment equal to two times the executive’s\n1809: annual base salary in effect at the time of the change in control (or two and one-half times if at the time of the terminating event the executive has 30 or more years of service with the Company and its successor), payable within thirty (30) days\n1810: of the date of termination. The closing of the merger will constitute a change in control for purposes of the change in control severance agreements. \n1811:  Under the terms of the change in control severance agreements, in the event that payments and benefits under the agreements, together with\n1812: other payments and benefits the executive may receive, would constitute an “excess parachute payment” under Section 280G of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended (“Section 280G”), the payments to the executive will be\n1813: reduced to the maximum amount that can be deducted by the Company under Section 280G (the “280G Threshold”). Assuming the merger is completed in 2016, the Company anticipates that severance payments to the named executive officers and\n1814: other executive officers under these agreements, together with the other amounts paid to such executives with respect to unvested equity awards, will exceed the 280G Threshold for each executive. Accordingly, the severance payments will be reduced\n1815: as noted in footnote 1 to the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47. \n1816:  In addition, each of\n1817: the change in control severance agreements contains confidentiality provisions that are enforceable beyond the termination of the executive’s employment and beyond the expiration of the change in control severance agreement. \n1818:  Supplemental Employment Agreements . Immediately prior to the execution of the merger agreement, the Company entered into a\n1819: Supplemental Employment Agreement with P. Scott Conti, the Company’s President and Chief Operating Officer and a named executive officer, and Charles D. Rennick, the Company’s Secretary and General Counsel (each a “supplemental\n1820: agreement”), the terms of which begin on the first business day following the date of termination of the executive’s employment from his current position. The supplemental agreement with Mr. Conti provides that he will provide reasonable\n1821: services as may be requested from him and that if his employment as President and Chief Operating Officer is terminated by the Company on or before May 31, 2018 (the “contract termination date”), the Company will employ Mr. Conti until the\n1822: contract termination date or the date on which he becomes employed with another taxpayer under the Railroad Retirement Act, whichever is earlier. As compensation for such employment, Mr. Conti will be paid $200 per calendar month. The supplemental\n1823: agreement may be terminated by the Company upon Mr. Conti’s death or for “cause” as defined in the supplemental agreement. The supplemental agreement with Mr. Rennick is identical except that it provides that if his employment as\n1824: Secretary and General Counsel is terminated by the Company on or before May 31, 2017, the Company will employ Mr. Rennick until May 31, 2017 or the date on which he becomes employed with another taxpayer under the Railroad Retirement Act, whichever\n1825: is earlier. \n1826:  45 \n1827:  Table of Contents \n1828:  Settlement of Executive Officers’ and Directors’ Equity-Based Awards \n1829:  The directors and executive officers of the Company hold stock options issued under the Company 2015 Equity Plan and the 1992 Stock Option Plan\n1830: and the executive officers of the Company hold restricted stock units issued under the 2015 Equity Plan. Mr. Eder does not hold any options or restricted stock units. \n1831:  Stock Options. All stock options issued to the Company’s directors and executive officers under the 1992 Stock Option\n1832: Plan are fully vested. Options granted to non-employee directors under the 2015 Equity Plan vest immediately prior to the 2017 annual shareholders meeting and options granted to executives under the 2015 Equity Plan vest in five annual installments\n1833: of 10% on January 18, 2017, 10% on January 18, 2018, 20% on January 18, 2019, 30% on January 18, 2020 and 30% on January 18, 2021, provided that any such options vest in full upon a change in control. As of the date of this proxy statement, the\n1834: Company’s directors and executive officers (as a group) hold options to acquire an aggregate of 53,368 shares of common stock, of which 18,368 are vested and 35,000 are unvested. Under the terms of the merger agreement, upon closing of the\n1835: merger, all stock options (whether vested or unvested) will be cancelled and the holder will receive, for each share subject to an option, cash equal to $25.00 less the exercise price for the option, net of all applicable withholding taxes. The\n1836: amounts payable to named executive officers with respect to unvested options are included in the column titled “Equity” in the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47 and detailed in footnote 2 to\n1837: such table. \n1838:  Restricted Stock Units. Restricted stock units granted to executives under the 2015 Equity Plan generally vest in\n1839: installments over a five year period commencing on January 18, 2017, provided certain Company performance criteria are satisfied. Under the terms of the merger agreement, as of the effective time of the merger, each outstanding restricted stock\n1840: unit, whether vested or unvested, shall be canceled and shall be converted automatically into the right to receive $25.00 for each share of common stock covered by such restricted stock unit, less any required withholdings. The Company’s\n1841: executive officers (as a group) hold 43,500 unvested restricted stock units. The amounts payable to executive officers with respect to the restricted stock units, assuming the completion of the merger, are included in the column titled\n1842: “Equity” in the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47 and detailed in footnote 2 to such table. \n1843:  Summary of Merger-Related Executive Compensation Arrangements \n1844:  The following table and related footnotes set forth the information required by Item 402(t) of Regulation\n1845:  S-K regarding the aggregate dollar value of the various elements of compensation that each named executive officer of the Company would receive that is based on or otherwise relates to the merger, assuming the\n1846: following: \n1847:  •\n1848:  the merger closed on September 16, 2016, the latest practicable date prior to the filing of this proxy statement; and \n1849:  •\n1850:  the employment of each of Messrs. Conti and. Rogers is terminated by the Company without cause or such executive resigned with good reason on the day immediately following the closing of the merger, thus triggering the\n1851: severance payments under the change in control severance agreements. \n1852:  Any changes in these assumptions or estimates as to\n1853: the closing date of the merger would affect the value of the acceleration of time-based options and restricted stock unit awards, which would impact the calculations under Section 280G, which, in turn, would affect the amounts shown in the following\n1854: table. The actual amounts payable under the change in control severance agreements will depend on the date of termination, the manner of the termination and the terms of the agreements in effect at such time. The calculations in the table below do\n1855: not include amounts to be paid with respect to options that are vested as of the date of the merger in accordance with their terms without regard to the merger. Furthermore, neither Mr. Conti nor Mr. Rogers will receive the severance payments set\n1856: forth under the “Cash” column of the table unless the Company terminates such executive’s employment without “cause” or the executive terminates his employment for “good reason” within two years following the\n1857: completion of the merger. \n1858:  46 \n1859:  Table of Contents \n1860:  Mr. Eder, the Company’s Chairman and Chief Executive Officer, will not receive any\n1861: compensation based on or relating to the merger. None of our named executive officers are entitled to any pension or non-qualified deferred compensation benefits enhancements, tax reimbursement payments, or any other form of compensation that is\n1862: based on or otherwise related to the merger. \n1863:  Potential Change in Control Compensation \n1864:  Name \n1865:  Cash (1) \n1866:  Equity (2) \n1867:  Total (3) \n1868:  Robert H. Eder,\n1869:  Chairman & Chief Executive Officer \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1870]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt, lines 1740-1869\n1740:  2.1\n1741:  ( 0.5\n1742:  ) \n1743:  0.2\n1744:  1.4\n1745:  2.8\n1746:  Unlevered Free Cash Flow (c) \n1747:  (1.7\n1748:  ) \n1749:  ( 0.5\n1750:  ) \n1751:  ( 0.1\n1752:  ) \n1753:  1.4\n1754:  1.9\n1755:  (a) \n1756:  Fiscal 2016 data represents only third and fourth quarter 2016 financial information. Costs related to the merger were not included in fiscal 2016 third and fourth quarter financial information. \n1757:  (b) \n1758:  Tax-Affected EBIT represents Earnings before interest and income taxes (“EBIT”) less cash taxes calculated by multiplying 35% marginal tax rate by EBIT after adding back items which are not tax deductible such\n1759: as non-deductible amortization. EBIT is a non-GAAP measure consisting of net income plus interest expense and provision for income taxes. \n1760:  (c) \n1761:  Unlevered Free Cash Flow represents Tax-Affected EBIT plus depreciation and amortization and minus capital\n1762: expenditures and any increase in net working capital. Capital expenditures for fiscal years 2017-2020 have been normalized at 83.6% of depreciation based on 2.5% inflation and a 15-year depreciation period. \n1763:  Delisting and Deregistration of Common Stock Following the Merger \n1764:  If the merger is completed, the Company’s common stock will be delisted from the NASDAQ and deregistered under the Exchange Act and we\n1765: will no longer file periodic reports with the SEC on account of the common stock. \n1766:  43 \n1767:  Table of Contents \n1768:  Interests of Directors and Executive Officers in the Merger \n1769:  In considering the recommendation of the Board that you vote to approve the merger agreement, you should be aware that directors and executive\n1770: officers of the Company have financial interests in the merger that may be different from, or in addition to, those of the Company’s shareholders generally. The independent members of the Board were aware of and considered these potential\n1771: interests, among other matters. \n1772:  As described in more detail below, these interests include certain payments and benefits that may be\n1773: provided to directors and executive officers of the Company upon completion of the merger or upon termination of their employment under certain circumstances following the merger, including accelerated vesting of stock options and restricted stock\n1774: units, cash severance in the case of a qualifying termination of employment in connection with or following a change in control (which will include the completion of the merger), and continued indemnification and insurance coverage under the merger\n1775: agreement. \n1776:  The dates and share prices used below to quantify these interests have been selected based upon applicable disclosure\n1777: requirements and are for illustrative purposes only. They do not necessarily reflect the dates on which certain events will occur and do not represent a projection about the future value of common stock. \n1778:  Share Ownership of Directors and Executive Officers \n1779:  As of September 16, 2016, the record date for the special meeting of the Company’s shareholders, the directors and executive officers of\n1780: the Company may be deemed to be the beneficial owners of 1,107,002 shares of common stock (excluding common stock issuable upon conversion of the preferred stock), representing 22.7% of the outstanding shares of common stock, and 500 shares of\n1781: preferred stock, representing 78.1% of the outstanding shares of preferred stock. \n1782:  Indemnification \n1783:  The merger agreement provides that the surviving corporation will indemnify all past and present officers and directors of the Company and its\n1784: subsidiaries for acts or omissions related to such person’s service as a director or officer of the Company or its subsidiaries occurring at or prior to the effective time of the merger to the fullest extent permitted by the RIBCA, any other\n1785: applicable law, the Company’s articles of incorporation or the Company’s bylaws, subject to any limitation imposed by law. \n1786:  Under the merger agreement, G&W has agreed that all rights to indemnification and all limitations of liability existing in favor of any\n1787: director or officer of the Company or any of its subsidiaries, as provided in the articles of incorporation and bylaws of the Company, similar governing documents of a Company subsidiary or in applicable law as in effect on the date of the merger\n1788: agreement with respect to matters occurring on or prior to the effective time of the merger will survive the merger and be assumed by the surviving corporation. \n1789:  For a period of six years following the effective time of the merger, the articles of incorporation and the bylaws of the surviving\n1790: corporation will contain provisions no less favorable with respect to indemnification, advancement of expenses and exculpation of former or present directors and officers than as set forth in the articles of incorporation and bylaws of the Company\n1791: as of the date of the merger agreement, which provisions will not be amended, repealed or otherwise modified for six years following the effective time of the merger in any manner that would adversely affect the rights thereunder, except as may be\n1792: required by the RIBCA. \n1793:  Directors’ and Officers’ Insurance \n1794:  The merger agreement provides for G&W to cause the Company to maintain in effect its directors’ and officers’ liability insurance\n1795: coverage existing prior to the effective time of the merger on terms with respect to such coverage and amount no less favorable than those of such current insurance coverage for acts or omissions occurring at or prior to the effective time of the\n1796: merger for a period of six years following the effective time of \n1797:  44 \n1798:  Table of Contents \n1799: the merger. Neither G&W nor the surviving corporation will be obligated to expend in any one year an amount in excess of 300% of the annual premium paid by the Company for such insurance on\n1800: the date of the merger agreement. If the annual premiums for such insurance coverage exceed such amount, G&W and the Company will be obligated to obtain a policy with the greatest coverage available for a cost not exceeding such amount. In lieu\n1801: of the foregoing insurance coverage, G&W has the option to purchase, prior to the effective time of the merger, six year “tail” insurance coverage that provides coverage identical in all material respects to the coverage described\n1802: above, provided that the premium is not more than 300% of the annual premium paid by the Company for such insurance on the date of the merger agreement. \n1803:  The indemnification and insurance provisions of the merger agreement described above are intended to benefit, and are enforceable by, the\n1804: indemnified persons and their respective heirs or representatives. \n1805:  Change in Control Benefits Under Current Company Agreements \n1806:  Change in Control Agreements with Company Executives. The Company has entered into change in control severance agreements\n1807: with its executive officers (other than Mr. Eder), including named executive officers P. Scott Conti and Frank K. Rogers. The change in control severance agreements provide that, if the executive’s employment is terminated by the Company\n1808: without “cause” or by the executive for “good reason” (each as defined in the agreements) within two years after a change in control, the executive is entitled to a lump sum cash payment equal to two times the executive’s\n1809: annual base salary in effect at the time of the change in control (or two and one-half times if at the time of the terminating event the executive has 30 or more years of service with the Company and its successor), payable within thirty (30) days\n1810: of the date of termination. The closing of the merger will constitute a change in control for purposes of the change in control severance agreements. \n1811:  Under the terms of the change in control severance agreements, in the event that payments and benefits under the agreements, together with\n1812: other payments and benefits the executive may receive, would constitute an “excess parachute payment” under Section 280G of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended (“Section 280G”), the payments to the executive will be\n1813: reduced to the maximum amount that can be deducted by the Company under Section 280G (the “280G Threshold”). Assuming the merger is completed in 2016, the Company anticipates that severance payments to the named executive officers and\n1814: other executive officers under these agreements, together with the other amounts paid to such executives with respect to unvested equity awards, will exceed the 280G Threshold for each executive. Accordingly, the severance payments will be reduced\n1815: as noted in footnote 1 to the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47. \n1816:  In addition, each of\n1817: the change in control severance agreements contains confidentiality provisions that are enforceable beyond the termination of the executive’s employment and beyond the expiration of the change in control severance agreement. \n1818:  Supplemental Employment Agreements . Immediately prior to the execution of the merger agreement, the Company entered into a\n1819: Supplemental Employment Agreement with P. Scott Conti, the Company’s President and Chief Operating Officer and a named executive officer, and Charles D. Rennick, the Company’s Secretary and General Counsel (each a “supplemental\n1820: agreement”), the terms of which begin on the first business day following the date of termination of the executive’s employment from his current position. The supplemental agreement with Mr. Conti provides that he will provide reasonable\n1821: services as may be requested from him and that if his employment as President and Chief Operating Officer is terminated by the Company on or before May 31, 2018 (the “contract termination date”), the Company will employ Mr. Conti until the\n1822: contract termination date or the date on which he becomes employed with another taxpayer under the Railroad Retirement Act, whichever is earlier. As compensation for such employment, Mr. Conti will be paid $200 per calendar month. The supplemental\n1823: agreement may be terminated by the Company upon Mr. Conti’s death or for “cause” as defined in the supplemental agreement. The supplemental agreement with Mr. Rennick is identical except that it provides that if his employment as\n1824: Secretary and General Counsel is terminated by the Company on or before May 31, 2017, the Company will employ Mr. Rennick until May 31, 2017 or the date on which he becomes employed with another taxpayer under the Railroad Retirement Act, whichever\n1825: is earlier. \n1826:  45 \n1827:  Table of Contents \n1828:  Settlement of Executive Officers’ and Directors’ Equity-Based Awards \n1829:  The directors and executive officers of the Company hold stock options issued under the Company 2015 Equity Plan and the 1992 Stock Option Plan\n1830: and the executive officers of the Company hold restricted stock units issued under the 2015 Equity Plan. Mr. Eder does not hold any options or restricted stock units. \n1831:  Stock Options. All stock options issued to the Company’s directors and executive officers under the 1992 Stock Option\n1832: Plan are fully vested. Options granted to non-employee directors under the 2015 Equity Plan vest immediately prior to the 2017 annual shareholders meeting and options granted to executives under the 2015 Equity Plan vest in five annual installments\n1833: of 10% on January 18, 2017, 10% on January 18, 2018, 20% on January 18, 2019, 30% on January 18, 2020 and 30% on January 18, 2021, provided that any such options vest in full upon a change in control. As of the date of this proxy statement, the\n1834: Company’s directors and executive officers (as a group) hold options to acquire an aggregate of 53,368 shares of common stock, of which 18,368 are vested and 35,000 are unvested. Under the terms of the merger agreement, upon closing of the\n1835: merger, all stock options (whether vested or unvested) will be cancelled and the holder will receive, for each share subject to an option, cash equal to $25.00 less the exercise price for the option, net of all applicable withholding taxes. The\n1836: amounts payable to named executive officers with respect to unvested options are included in the column titled “Equity” in the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47 and detailed in footnote 2 to\n1837: such table. \n1838:  Restricted Stock Units. Restricted stock units granted to executives under the 2015 Equity Plan generally vest in\n1839: installments over a five year period commencing on January 18, 2017, provided certain Company performance criteria are satisfied. Under the terms of the merger agreement, as of the effective time of the merger, each outstanding restricted stock\n1840: unit, whether vested or unvested, shall be canceled and shall be converted automatically into the right to receive $25.00 for each share of common stock covered by such restricted stock unit, less any required withholdings. The Company’s\n1841: executive officers (as a group) hold 43,500 unvested restricted stock units. The amounts payable to executive officers with respect to the restricted stock units, assuming the completion of the merger, are included in the column titled\n1842: “Equity” in the table entitled “Potential Change in Control Compensation” on page 47 and detailed in footnote 2 to such table. \n1843:  Summary of Merger-Related Executive Compensation Arrangements \n1844:  The following table and related footnotes set forth the information required by Item 402(t) of Regulation\n1845:  S-K regarding the aggregate dollar value of the various elements of compensation that each named executive officer of the Company would receive that is based on or otherwise relates to the merger, assuming the\n1846: following: \n1847:  •\n1848:  the merger closed on September 16, 2016, the latest practicable date prior to the filing of this proxy statement; and \n1849:  •\n1850:  the employment of each of Messrs. Conti and. Rogers is terminated by the Company without cause or such executive resigned with good reason on the day immediately following the closing of the merger, thus triggering the\n1851: severance payments under the change in control severance agreements. \n1852:  Any changes in these assumptions or estimates as to\n1853: the closing date of the merger would affect the value of the acceleration of time-based options and restricted stock unit awards, which would impact the calculations under Section 280G, which, in turn, would affect the amounts shown in the following\n1854: table. The actual amounts payable under the change in control severance agreements will depend on the date of termination, the manner of the termination and the terms of the agreements in effect at such time. The calculations in the table below do\n1855: not include amounts to be paid with respect to options that are vested as of the date of the merger in accordance with their terms without regard to the merger. Furthermore, neither Mr. Conti nor Mr. Rogers will receive the severance payments set\n1856: forth under the “Cash” column of the table unless the Company terminates such executive’s employment without “cause” or the executive terminates his employment for “good reason” within two years following the\n1857: completion of the merger. \n1858:  46 \n1859:  Table of Contents \n1860:  Mr. Eder, the Company’s Chairman and Chief Executive Officer, will not receive any\n1861: compensation based on or relating to the merger. None of our named executive officers are entitled to any pension or non-qualified deferred compensation benefits enhancements, tax reimbursement payments, or any other form of compensation that is\n1862: based on or otherwise related to the merger. \n1863:  Potential Change in Control Compensation \n1864:  Name \n1865:  Cash (1) \n1866:  Equity (2) \n1867:  Total (3) \n1868:  Robert H. Eder,\n1869:  Chairman & Chief Executive Officer \n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 1870]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\": 130, \"offset\": 1740, \"path\": \"/tmp/opencode/pxw_text.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1789824412686,"end":1789824413100}}}}
